   "text": "An official website of the United States government\nSearch or ask anything\nAsk DOI Anything - How Can We Help?\nFind national parks near me\nDo I need a reservation to enter a national park?\nHow can my class take a virtual field trip to a national park?\nCan I bring my dog to a national park?\nSkip to global NPS navigation\nSkip to the main content\nSkip to the footer section\nNational Park Service\nOpen\nMenu\nARTICLE\nTaunton Wild and Scenic River\nOn March 30, 2009, the Taunton was designated as a Partnership Wild and Scenic RIver. Located in southeastern Massachusetts, the Taunton river flows for approximately 40 miles. The Taunton drainage area is the second largest in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts and contains twenty cities and towns, with portions of twenty-three others. Within this area is a variety of land types including wetlands, estuaries, and agricultural use leading to a vast amount of biodiversity within the river and watershed. To learn more about Partnership Wild and Scenic Rivers and the designation process, visit the PWSR Toolkit.\nIN THIS ARTICLE:\nPartner Spotlight\nConservation\nWater Quality\nDesignation Origin Story\nReturn to Main Article\nTAGS\ntaunton wild and scenic river\nLast updated: March 17, 2021\nWas this page helpful?\nYes\nNo\nAn official form of the United States government. Provided by Touchpoints\nExperience Your America.\nDownload the NPS app to navigate the parks on the go.\nSearch\nAccessibility\nPrivacy Policy\nFOIA\nNotices\nContact The National Park Service\nNPS FAQ\nNo Fear Act\nDisclaimer\nVulnerability Disclosure Policy\nUSA.gov\nOffice of Civil Rights\nLooking for U.S. government information and services? Visit USA.gov",
